UgzFDfqUs-P14giooFp4AaABAg,,False,@Maraaha55,"Thank you, gentlemen, I loved your thoughtful and warm conversation. It made me thoughtful and reflective too. The idea that though people who saw things as black or white, have the louder voices, so they seem to be more numerous, but in fact there are far more people in the middle, who might be more grey is of course true. However I felt that that description might sound insipid or boring, which makes me uncomfortable.
People who either don't have a strong opinion, or choose not to share their opinion are far far more numerous. Sometimes they feel diffident about their thoughts, but probably it's more likely that they have other concerns, like people or things or activities that they love far more than just airing their own thoughts on the abstract categorisation of any kind of music.
When you recognise that, then perhaps it might be more accurate to think of those people whose thoughts are neither black nor white, as colourful: full of blue, pink, gold, red, violet, auburn, green and even brown, rather than what I am sure in real life would actually be a range of silver greys. I find that thought comforting.",1,2023-02-24T14:46:10Z,2023-02-24T14:46:10Z
